# UI/UX Design Rules

When writing UI code, think like an experienced UI/UX designer. Visual quality, hierarchy, and user experience are equally important as functionality.

## Core Principles

- **Visual hierarchy:** The user's eye should go to important elements first. Guide with size, color, and spacing
- **Consistency:** Similar elements should look similar. Spacing, color, and typography must be consistent
- **Whitespace:** Do not write cramped UI. Use adequate padding/margin; give the design room to breathe
- **Feedback:** Every user action must have a visual response (hover, focus, loading, success, error)
- **Mobile-first:** All components must be mobile-responsive; use breakpoints in `sm → md → lg` order

## Typography

- Use at least `font-semibold` for headings
- Body text between `text-sm` and `text-base` with adequate `leading` (line height)
- Use `text-muted-foreground` for secondary/helper text (not raw gray)
- Text hierarchy: h1 > h2 > h3 — each level must have a distinct visual difference

## Color and Contrast

- Use shadcn/ui tokens (`background`, `foreground`, `muted`, `accent`, `destructive`, etc.)
- Do not use raw hex colors or fixed colors like `text-gray-*` — it breaks theme compatibility
- For errors use `destructive`; for success use dark-mode-compatible colors like `text-green-600 dark:text-green-400`

## Interaction States

Every interactive element must handle:
- `hover:` — noticeable but not overdone
- `focus-visible:` — accessible `ring` style
- `disabled:` — `opacity-50 cursor-not-allowed`
- Loading: skeleton or spinner; never leave content blank

## Spacing System

- Component inner padding: between `p-3` and `p-6`
- Gap between elements: between `gap-2` and `gap-4` (use `space-y-2` for lists)
- Page container: `max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4`
- Between sections: `py-8` or `mb-8`

## Form Design

- Label always above the input
- Error message below the input, as `text-destructive text-sm`
- Required fields must be marked with `*`
- Submit button must show `disabled` + spinner in loading state

## Accessibility

- All interactive elements must have an `aria-label` or visible label
- Icon-only buttons must have an `aria-label`
- Color must not be the sole carrier of information (support with icons)
- Do not use `<div onClick>` instead of `<button>`

## Empty States

- Do not leave empty lists — show a descriptive message and an action button
- Use skeleton loaders instead of sudden content appearances

## Bad Practices

- Using too many different colors
- Inconsistent border-radius (pick either `rounded-md` or `rounded-lg` and stick to it)
- Fixed widths that overflow on small screens (like `w-[400px]`)
- Writing all text in uppercase
- Avoiding inline error messages in favor of always using alerts/toasts
